# CCOT -> W3C OWL-Time mapping axioms (subject = CCOT class or the BFO class that CCOT
# builds on). Project 3, steps 4 and 8. Review notes are in mapping-decisions.md.
#
# Targeted vs global reasoning: only the two BFO parents are mapped. The reasoner then
# pushes every CCOT interval (Day, Hour, Week, Morning, ...) under time:ProperInterval and
# every CCOT instant (Time of Day, Reference Time, Unix Temporal Instant, ...) under
# time:Instant, so there is no need to assert 20+ leaf-level axioms by hand.

@prefix owl:  <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#> .
@prefix rdfs: <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .
@prefix obo:  <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/> .
@prefix cco:  <https://www.commoncoreontologies.org/> .
@prefix time: <http://www.w3.org/2006/time#> .

<http://example.org/ontology-tradecraft/project-3/ccot-mapping> a owl:Ontology ;
    rdfs:comment "CCOT-to-OWL-Time alignment axioms whose subject is a CCOT/BFO class." .

# BFO temporal interval (continuous, no gaps) always has distinct begin and end:
# OWL-Time ProperInterval = "a temporal entity with non-zero extent ... the value of
# the beginning and end are different".
obo:BFO_0000202 rdfs:subClassOf time:ProperInterval .

# BFO temporal instant ("zero-dimensional temporal region that has no proper temporal
# part") = OWL-Time Instant ("a temporal entity with zero extent or duration").
# Converse asserted in to-mapping.ttl.
obo:BFO_0000203 rdfs:subClassOf time:Instant .

# Gregorian Day / Gregorian Year are calendar-aligned intervals in the Gregorian calendar:
# exactly what OWL-Time DateTimeInterval ("defined using the multi-element
# DateTimeDescription", Gregorian by default) describes. Julian Day/Year are NOT mapped:
# DateTimeDescription is fixed to the Gregorian calendar.
cco:ont00000435 rdfs:subClassOf time:DateTimeInterval .   # Gregorian Day
cco:ont00000425 rdfs:subClassOf time:DateTimeInterval .   # Gregorian Year
